# ---- Terraform ----
**/.terraform/*
# NOT .terraform.lock.hcl — lock files are COMMITTED, deliberately.
#
# Ignoring them meant every `terraform init` on a fresh clone or CI runner re-resolved
# providers from scratch. With `agents/bedrock` constrained at `>= 5.40` — no upper bound —
# that silently picks up the next major on release, inside a workflow that ran
# `apply -auto-approve` with no plan. It is both a reproducibility hole and a supply-chain
# one, and it was the only entry in this file that was actively wrong.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
# Saved plans. A .tfplan stores every variable value VERBATIM, including `sensitive = true`
# ones — `sensitive` redacts console output, not the file. A local `make plan` was one
# `git add .` away from committing DATABRICKS_CLIENT_SECRET.
tfplan
*.tfplan
plan.txt
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
# ...EXCEPT auto-loaded environment config. `*.tfvars` is ignored to stop credentials
# reaching git, but non-secret deployment configuration (which region, whether MSK is
# provisioned) is exactly what IaC is FOR and must be version-controlled — otherwise CI
# silently applies the defaults instead of what the deployment actually chose.
# Secrets still never belong here: they go via TF_VAR_ environment variables.
!*.auto.tfvars
crash.log
crash.*.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# ---- Databricks ----
.databricks/
.databricks-login.json

